<section xml:id="section-representations">
  <title>Group Actions and Representations</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this last section on group theory, I want to introduce one
      last definition that is central to group theory: groups actions.
      I don't want to get too deeply into the technique, but I want to
      use the idea of a group action to talk more about the
      classification of groups and the understanding of their
      structure. That will lead to representations, a major idea in
      groups theory. But first the main definition.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-actions">
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>S</m> be a set. An
          <term>action</term> of the group on the set is defined by
          the following properties. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              For all <m>g \in G</m> and for all <m>s \in S</m>, there
              is an operation (written multiplicatively by default)
              <m>gs</m> that produces a new element of the set. 
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>g,h \in G</m> and <m>s \in S</m>, the the
              operation on the set satisfies this equation..
              <me>
                (gh)s = g(hs)
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              The identity elements in <m>G</m>, <m>e</m>, satisfies
              <me>
                es - s
              </me>
              for all <m>s \in S</m>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <ul>
            There is a function <m>f: G \times S \rightarrow S</m>
            with the same properties as the operaation defined above. 
          </ul>
          <ul>
            There is a homomorphism <m>:\phi G \rightarrow Sym(S)</m>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      All three versions of the definition capture the idea that the
      group is doing something to the elements of the set: permuting
      them, changing them, acting on them. We've already covered
      examples of groups that have this structure by definition: the
      dihedral groups and other geometric symmetry groups act on their
      geometric figures, the permutation groups act as symmetries. The
      definition here is generalizing these examples. Another groups
      which is not necessarily defined as functions on a set maybe be
      realized as functions on a set in this way. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Let me run through some examples to make this more clear. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        As I just mentioned, any groups that is explicitly defined as
        symmetries or permutations are already groups actions. The
        dihedral groups are group actions on the sets of vertices of
        polygons. The permutation groups <m>S_n</m> are group actions
        on <m>\{1, 2, \dots, n\}</m>. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
